[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Granular Field-Level and Structural Validation Rules in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Firebase Firestore Security Rules allow you to secure your data while simultaneously enforcing strict structural and field-level validation. Relying only on client-side validation is a major security risk. By implementing robust rules on the server side, you ensure that incoming documents adhere to exact schemas, data types, and business logic constraints."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Validating Document Structure and Mandatory Fields"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To enforce a specific structure, your rules must verify that incoming documents contain all required keys and do not contain unauthorized extra fields. You can achieve this by checking the keys of the request.resource.data map."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /users/{userId} {
      allow create: if request.auth != null 
        && request.resource.data.keys().hasAll(['email', 'username', 'createdAt'])
        && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['email', 'username', 'createdAt', 'bio']);
    }
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Field-Level Type Validation"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Ensuring that fields hold the correct data type is critical for maintaining application stability. Firestore security rules provide built-in type checking functions such as is(), int(), string(), float(), bool(), and list()."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"string: Validated using request.resource.data.email is string"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"number: Validated using request.resource.data.age is int or float"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"boolean: Validated using request.resource.data.isActive is bool"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"timestamp: Validated using request.resource.data.createdAt is timestamp"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Implementing Complex Conditional Validation"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can combine structural validation with business logic. For instance, you may want to ensure that fields like 'createdAt' match the exact server timestamp and cannot be manipulated by the client."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"match /posts/{postId} {
  allow create: if request.auth != null
    && request.resource.data.authorId == request.auth.uid
    && request.resource.data.title is string
    && request.resource.data.title.size() > 0
    && request.resource.data.title.size() <= 100
    && request.resource.data.createdAt == request.time;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Validating Updates and Immutability"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"When updating documents, you often want to ensure that certain fields (like an owner ID or creation timestamp) remain immutable. Use resource.data to check existing values against request.resource.data."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"match /articles/{articleId} {
  allow update: if request.auth != null
    && resource.data.authorId == request.auth.uid
    && request.resource.data.authorId == resource.data.authorId
    && request.resource.data.updatedAt == request.time
    && request.resource.data.title is string;
}"},{"order":18,"type":"heading","text":"Summary"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"By leveraging keys().hasAll(), keys().hasOnly(), type checking functions, and request.time comparisons, you can create a bulletproof schema validation layer directly inside your Firebase Firestore Security Rules."}]
